### What is TransactionTemplate?

`TransactionTemplate` is a Spring class that provides programmatic transaction management. Instead of declaring transaction boundaries with the `@Transactional` annotation, you wrap the transactional code inside a callback and the template manages begin, commit, and rollback. It wraps a `PlatformTransactionManager` and is thread-safe once configured.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    private final TransactionTemplate txTemplate;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public TransferService(
            PlatformTransactionManager txManager,
            AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.txTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    public BigDecimal transferAndGetBalance(
            String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        return txTemplate.execute(status -> {
            Account sender = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
            Account receiver = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId);
            sender.debit(amount);
            receiver.credit(amount);
            accountRepo.save(sender);
            accountRepo.save(receiver);
            return sender.getBalance();
        });
    }
}
```

### Why use TransactionTemplate instead of @Transactional?

There are three main reasons to choose programmatic over declarative.

First, batch processing. Each `execute` call is an independent transaction. In a batch job, you can commit every chunk of 500 accounts separately. If chunk 3 fails, chunks 1 and 2 are already committed.

Second, self-invocation. With `@Transactional`, calling a transactional method on `this` bypasses the AOP proxy and the annotation is silently ignored. `TransactionTemplate` is a direct method call, so there is no proxy to bypass.

Third, fine-grained boundaries. You can mix transactional and non-transactional work in one method, placing the transaction boundary around only the database operations.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class MixedWorkService {

    private final TransactionTemplate txTemplate;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;
    private final AuditClient auditClient;

    public MixedWorkService(
            PlatformTransactionManager txManager,
            AccountRepository accountRepo,
            AuditClient auditClient) {
        this.txTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
        this.auditClient = auditClient;
    }

    public void deposit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Non-transactional: external API call
        auditClient.logAttempt(accountId, amount);

        // Transactional: database work only
        txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> {
            Account account = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(accountId);
            account.credit(amount);
            accountRepo.save(account);
        });

        // Non-transactional: notification
        auditClient.logSuccess(accountId, amount);
    }
}
```

### How do execute and executeWithoutResult work?

The `execute` method takes a `TransactionCallback<T>` (a functional interface that returns T) and returns the callback's result. The template begins a transaction, runs the callback, and commits on normal return. If the callback throws a RuntimeException, the template rolls back and re-throws.

The `executeWithoutResult` method takes a `Consumer<TransactionStatus>` and returns void. It was added in Spring 5.2 to avoid the pattern of `execute(status -> { ...; return null; })`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class AccountService {

    private final TransactionTemplate txTemplate;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public AccountService(
            PlatformTransactionManager txManager,
            AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.txTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    // execute: returns a value from inside the transaction
    public BigDecimal getBalanceInTx(String accountId) {
        return txTemplate.execute(status -> {
            Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId);
            return account.getBalance();
        });
    }

    // executeWithoutResult: void operation
    public void freezeAccount(String accountId) {
        txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> {
            Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId);
            account.setStatus("FROZEN");
            accountRepo.save(account);
        });
    }
}
```

```text
Balance for SAV2001: 150000.00
Account SAV2001 frozen successfully
```

### How does TransactionStatus.setRollbackOnly() work?

The `TransactionStatus` object passed into the callback allows you to force a rollback without throwing an exception. Call `status.setRollbackOnly()` when a business rule fails but you do not want to throw. This is useful for conditional rollback based on runtime data.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class WithdrawalService {

    private final TransactionTemplate txTemplate;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public WithdrawalService(
            PlatformTransactionManager txManager,
            AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.txTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    public boolean withdraw(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        return Boolean.TRUE.equals(txTemplate.execute(status -> {
            Account account = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(accountId);
            if (account.getBalance().compareTo(amount) < 0) {
                status.setRollbackOnly();
                return false; // insufficient funds, rollback quietly
            }
            account.debit(amount);
            accountRepo.save(account);
            return true; // committed
        }));
    }
}
```

### How does TransactionTemplate fix self-invocation?

The self-invocation problem occurs when a method in a bean calls another `@Transactional` method on `this`. Because `this` points to the real bean, not the proxy, the annotation is silently ignored. `TransactionTemplate` solves this because it is a direct method call that does not depend on a proxy.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class BatchSettlementService {

    private final TransactionTemplate txTemplate;
    private final TradeRepository tradeRepo;

    public BatchSettlementService(
            PlatformTransactionManager txManager,
            TradeRepository tradeRepo) {
        this.txTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        this.tradeRepo = tradeRepo;
    }

    // Each trade settles in its own transaction
    // No proxy needed: self-invocation is not a problem
    public void settleAll() {
        List<Trade> pending = tradeRepo.findPending();
        int settled = 0;
        for (Trade trade : pending) {
            try {
                txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> {
                    trade.settle();
                    tradeRepo.save(trade);
                });
                settled++;
            } catch (Exception e) {
                System.out.println("Trade " + trade.getId() + " failed: " + e.getMessage());
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Settled " + settled + " of " + pending.size());
    }
}
```

```text
Settled 498 of 500
```

### How do you configure TransactionTemplate?

You can set propagation, isolation, timeout, and readOnly on the template using setter methods. Configure it once in the constructor or as a Spring bean.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.TransactionDefinition;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate;

@Configuration
public class TxTemplateConfig {

    @Bean
    public TransactionTemplate defaultTxTemplate(PlatformTransactionManager txManager) {
        return new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
    }

    @Bean
    public TransactionTemplate batchTxTemplate(PlatformTransactionManager txManager) {
        TransactionTemplate template = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        template.setPropagationBehavior(
            TransactionDefinition.PROPAGATION_REQUIRES_NEW);
        template.setTimeout(30);
        return template;
    }

    @Bean
    public TransactionTemplate readOnlyTxTemplate(PlatformTransactionManager txManager) {
        TransactionTemplate template = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        template.setReadOnly(true);
        template.setTimeout(60);
        return template;
    }
}
```

### How does TransactionTemplate compare to @Transactional?

| Aspect | @Transactional | TransactionTemplate |
|--------|----------------|---------------------|
| Style | Declarative (annotation) | Programmatic (code) |
| Boundary | Entire method | Lambda block |
| Proxy required | Yes (AOP) | No |
| Self-invocation | Broken | Works |
| Multiple TXs in one method | Not possible | Each execute() is independent |
| Rollback control | Exception propagation | Exception or setRollbackOnly() |
| Configuration | Annotation attributes | Setter methods |
| Readability | Clean, one line | More verbose |
| Thread safety | N/A (proxy-based) | Thread-safe once configured |

### What are common mistakes with TransactionTemplate?

#### Mistake 1: creating a new template per call

```java
// WRONG: creating template every time wastes resources
public void deposit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
    TransactionTemplate tt = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
    tt.executeWithoutResult(status -> { ... });
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: create once in constructor, reuse
private final TransactionTemplate txTemplate;

public DepositService(PlatformTransactionManager txManager) {
    this.txTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
}

public void deposit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
    txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> { ... });
}
```

#### Mistake 2: catching exceptions inside the callback

```java
// WRONG: swallowing the exception prevents rollback
txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> {
    try {
        accountRepo.debit(accountId, amount);
        externalService.charge(amount);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        log.error("Failed", e);
        // transaction commits because exception is swallowed
    }
});
```

```java
// CORRECT: let exception propagate, or use setRollbackOnly
txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> {
    try {
        accountRepo.debit(accountId, amount);
        externalService.charge(amount);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        status.setRollbackOnly();
        throw new RuntimeException("Transfer failed", e);
    }
});
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- `TransactionTemplate` wraps `PlatformTransactionManager`. Using the manager directly with `getTransaction()`, `commit()`, and `rollback()` is possible but rarely needed.
- In reactive applications (WebFlux), use `TransactionalOperator` instead of `TransactionTemplate` because reactive code does not have ThreadLocal-bound connections.
- The template's propagation default is `PROPAGATION_REQUIRED`, same as `@Transactional`. For batch jobs, set `PROPAGATION_REQUIRES_NEW` so each chunk gets its own independent transaction.
- Combine `TransactionTemplate` with `@Retryable` from Spring Retry for automatic retry on transient database errors (deadlocks, serialization failures).
- If you need both declarative and programmatic in the same service, use declarative for the main methods and programmatic for internal helpers that need fine-grained control.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Retryable;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate;
import org.springframework.dao.TransientDataAccessException;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class ResilientTransferService {

    private final TransactionTemplate txTemplate;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public ResilientTransferService(
            PlatformTransactionManager txManager,
            AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.txTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Retryable(retryFor = TransientDataAccessException.class, maxAttempts = 3)
    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> {
            Account sender = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
            Account receiver = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId);
            sender.debit(amount);
            receiver.credit(amount);
        });
    }
}
```
